I see that `SubProcessAdapter.submit` always registers with `kind: "impl"` - this is hardcoded. But in `dispatch-reviewer.ts`, it's calling `reviewerAdapter.submit(input)` which would register with `kind: "impl"`.

But looking at `dispatch-reviewer.ts`, it creates the adapter with `handleKind: "review"`:
```typescript
const reviewerAdapter = new SubProcessAdapter({
  broker,
  handleKind: "review",
  ...
});


```

But `SubProcessAdapterOpts` doesn't have a `handleKind` option. Looking at the actual SubProcessAdapter implementation, it always registers with `kind: "impl"`. So the `handleKind: "review"` in the previous agent's dispatch-reviewer.ts won't actually work - it's not a valid option.

This is a bug in the dispatch-reviewer.ts that was created. The `SubProcessAdapter` doesn't support `handleKind`. Looking at the ABI types, `HandleKind` only supports `"impl"` and `"review-fix"`, not `"review"`, so this configuration is invalid.

The reap-loop also doesn't have a handler for `kind === "review"` completions - it only handles `"impl"` and `"review-fix"`. So even if the invalid `handleKind` were somehow accepted, the completion wouldn't be processed correctly. But T2's subtasks mention adding `kind=review` routing in reap-loop.ts, which suggests this is part of what needs to be implemented.
